.TH "CANVASRENDERINGCONTEXT2D.LANG" "3JS" "May 11, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CanvasRenderingContext2D.lang \- CanvasRenderingContext2D: lang property
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]CanvasRenderingContext2D.lang\f[B]\f[R] property of the
Canvas 2D API gets or sets the language of the canvas drawing context.
.SH VALUE
The \f[CR]lang\f[R] property can take one of the following string
values:
.IP \(bu 2
A BCP 47 language tag representing the language of the canvas context.
.IP \(bu 2
The string \f[CR]inherit\f[R], in which case the language is inherited
from the \f[CR]lang\f[R] attribute of the originating
\f[CR]<canvas>\f[R] element or the nearest available ancestor with an
explicit \f[CR]lang\f[R] set.
.IP \(bu 2
An empty string (\f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]), which can be set to specify that
the canvas context has no language.
.PP
The default value is \f[CR]inherit\f[R].
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sometimes, you need to set a language for a canvas rendering context so
that it knows how to render language\-dependent features: for example,
some fonts have certain characters rendered differently in different
languages.
An on\-screen canvas context (\f[CR]CanvasRenderingContext2D\f[R]) is
always associated with a particular \f[CR]<canvas>\f[R] element, so
whenever you render content using it, it can derive the language from
the value of the \f[CR]<canvas>\f[R] element\(cqs \f[CR]lang\f[R]
attribute.
.PP
However, an off\-screen canvas context
(\f[CR]OffscreenCanvasRenderingContext2D\f[R]) renders its content
before it is associated with a \f[CR]<canvas>\f[R] element, so it
can\(cqt derive a rendering language from the \f[CR]lang\f[R] attribute
of the \f[CR]<canvas>\f[R] element.
The \f[CR]lang\f[R] property addresses this issue, allowing you to set a
language directly on a canvas rendering context, whether you are using
an on\-screen or off\-screen canvas.
.SS The \f[CR]inherit\f[R] value
When the \f[CR]inherit\f[R] value is used, the language of the canvas
context is inherited from the \f[CR]lang\f[R] attribute of the
nearest\-available HTML source:
.IP \(bu 2
In the case of an on\-screen context, or an off\-screen context that was
transferred from an on\-screen context, this will be the originating
\f[CR]<canvas>\f[R] element, provided it has a valid \f[CR]lang\f[R]
attribute set.
.IP \(bu 2
If a \f[CR]lang\f[R] attribute is not available on an associated
\f[CR]<canvas>\f[R] element, which could be the case for an on\- or
off\-screen context, this will be the nearest available ancestor with an
explicit \f[CR]lang\f[R] set, which is commonly the document root.
.PP
Due to technical limitations, the \f[CR]inherit\f[R] value behaves
differently for on\-screen and off\-screen canvases:
.IP \(bu 2
For on\-screen canvases, the \f[CR]lang\f[R] value of the context is
inherited when the associated \f[CR]CanvasRenderingContext2D\f[R] object
is first created, and is updated dynamically if the \f[CR]lang\f[R]
attribute of the associated canvas is updated (either directly or by
inheritance).
.IP \(bu 2
For off\-screen canvases, the \f[CR]lang\f[R] value is inherited when
the associated \f[CR]OffscreenCanvasRenderingContext2D\f[R] object is
first created \(lqas a snapshot\(rq; subsequent updates to the
\f[CR]lang\f[R] attribute from which the offscreen context inherited its
value do not change its \f[CR]lang\f[R] attribute.
For this reason, the language of an off\-screen canvas can only be
changed by setting its \f[CR]lang\f[R] value explicitly.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] canvasElem = document.querySelector(\(dqcanvas\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] ctx = canvasElem.getContext(\(dq2d\(dq);

\f[I]// Get context language; returns \(dqinherit\(dq by default\f[R]
console.log(ctx.lang);

\f[I]// Set context language\f[R]
ctx.lang = \(dqen\(dq;
\f[I]// Logs \(dqen\(dq\f[R]
console.log(ctx.lang);
.EE
.SS Demonstrating canvas context localization support
In this example, we render a text string to a 2D canvas context in a
particular font that has language\-dependent ligatures.
We allow the canvas context\(cqs language to be adjusted so you can see
the difference in rendering.
.SS HTML
The HTML features a \f[CR]<select>\f[R] element that allows you to
choose a language \(em \f[CR]en\f[R] (English) or \f[CR]tr\f[R]
(Turkish) \(em and a \f[CR]<canvas>\f[R] element to render to.
.PP
\f[CR]html live\-example___canvas\-l10n <p>   <label for=\(dqlang\(dq>Choose language:</label>   <select id=\(dqlang\(dq name=\(dqlang\(dq>     <option>en</option>     <option>tr</option>   </select> </p> <canvas></canvas>\f[R]
.SS JavaScript
In the JavaScript, we first grab references to the \f[CR]<canvas>\f[R]
element, its \f[CR]CanvasRenderingContext2D\f[R], and the
\f[CR]<select>\f[R] element, then load the language\-dependant font
using the CSS Font Loading API.
Once the font is loaded, we run an \f[CR]init()\f[R] function.
This function defines another function \(em \f[CR]drawText()\f[R], which
draws some text to the canvas context that uses the loaded font, adds a
\f[CR]change\f[R] event listener to the \f[CR]<select>\f[R] element,
then calls \f[CR]drawText()\f[R] so that the text is immediately drawn
to the canvas when the page first loads.
.PP
\(ga\(ga\(gajs live\-example___canvas\-l10n const canvasElem =
document.querySelector(\(lqcanvas\(rq); const ctx =
canvasElem.getContext(\(lq2d\(rq);
.PP
const selectElem = document.querySelector(\(lqselect\(rq);
.PP
const latoMediumFontFace = new FontFace( // Lato\-Medium is a font with
language specific ligatures \(lqLato\-Medium\(rq,
`url(\(lqhttps://mdn.github.io/shared\-assets/fonts/Lato\-Medium.ttf\(rq)',
);
.PP
latoMediumFontFace.load().then((font) => { document.fonts.add(font);
init(); });
.PP
function init() { function drawText() { ctx.clearRect(0, 0,
canvasElem.width, canvasElem.height); ctx.font = \(lq30px
Lato\-Medium\(rq; ctx.color = \(lqblack\(rq; ctx.fillText(\(lqfinish
crafting\(rq, 50, 100); }
.PP
selectElem.addEventListener(\(lqchange\(rq, () => {
document.documentElement.lang = selectElem.value; drawText(); });
.PP
drawText(); }
.IP
.EX

When the \(ga<select>\(ga value is changed, the \(gachange\(ga event handler function fires, which:

\- Sets the value of the \(ga<html>\(ga element\(aqs [\(galang\(ga](/en\-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Global_attributes/lang) attribute to the \(ga<select>\(ga element value, effectively changing the language of the document.
\- Runs the \(gadrawText()\(ga function. The \(gaCanvasRenderingContext2D.lang\(ga property is set to \(gainherit\(ga by default, therefore the canvas context inherits the language of the document.

### Result

The example is rendered as follows:



Try changing the document language using the \(ga<select>\(ga element. When the language is set to English, the font will be rendered with the \(dqfi\(dq ligature. However, when it is set to Turkish, the font will be rendered without the \(dqfi\(dq ligature, because that locale doesn\(aqt include it.

## Language support for offscreen canvases

This example is the similar to the previous example, except that the font is rendered to an \(gaOffscreenCanvasRenderingContext2D\(ga then the resulting bitmap is transferred to the on\-screen \(ga<canvas>\(ga to display.

In addition, because an inherited off\-screen canvas language is only set once, and not dynamically updated if the inherited \(galang\(ga attribute value is changed, we explicitly set the \(galang\(ga property on the \(gaOffscreenCanvasRenderingContext2D\(ga instead.

### HTML

\(ga\(ga\(gahtml live\-example___offscreen\-l10n
<p>
  <label for=\(dqlang\(dq>Choose language:</label>
  <select id=\(dqlang\(dq name=\(dqlang\(dq>
    <option>en</option>
    <option>tr</option>
  </select>
</p>
<canvas></canvas>
.EE
.SS JavaScript
The JavaScript works in the same way as the previous example, except
that:
.IP \(bu 2
The on\-screen canvas context is defined as an
\f[CR]ImageBitmapRenderingContext\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
We define a new \f[CR]OffscreenCanvasRenderingContext2D\f[R] to draw the
text onto, transfer the result to a bitmap using
\f[CR]transferToImageBitmap()\f[R], then render it on the
\f[CR]<canvas>\f[R] using \f[CR]transferFromImageBitmap()\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
When the \f[CR]<select>\f[R] value is changed, we update the
\f[CR]lang\f[R] property directly on the
\f[CR]OffscreenCanvasRenderingContext2D\f[R] instead of changing the
\f[CR]<html>\f[R] \f[CR]lang\f[R] attribute value.
.PP
\(ga\(ga\(gajs live\-example___offscreen\-l10n const canvasElem =
document.querySelector(\(lqcanvas\(rq); const ctx =
canvasElem.getContext(\(lqbitmaprenderer\(rq);
.PP
const offscreen = new OffscreenCanvas(canvasElem.width,
canvasElem.height); const offscreenCtx =
offscreen.getContext(\(lq2d\(rq);
.PP
const selectElem = document.querySelector(\(lqselect\(rq);
.PP
const latoMediumFontFace = new FontFace( // Lato\-Medium is a font with
language specific ligatures.
\(lqLato\-Medium\(rq,
`url(\(lqhttps://mdn.github.io/shared\-assets/fonts/Lato\-Medium.ttf\(rq)',
);
.PP
latoMediumFontFace.load().then((font) => { document.fonts.add(font);
init(); });
.PP
function init() { function drawText() { offscreenCtx.clearRect(0, 0,
canvasElem.width, canvasElem.height); offscreenCtx.lang =
selectElem.value; offscreenCtx.font = \(lq30px Lato\-Medium\(rq;
offscreenCtx.color = \(lqblack\(rq; offscreenCtx.fillText(\(lqfinish
crafting\(rq, 50, 100);
.IP
.EX
const bitmap = offscreen.transferToImageBitmap();
ctx.transferFromImageBitmap(bitmap);
.EE
.PP
}
.PP
selectElem.addEventListener(\(lqchange\(rq, () => { drawText(); });
.PP
drawText(); } \(ga\(ga\(ga
